WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.777
This slide gives us the workflow we will follow throughout the module.

00:00:06.777 --> 00:00:08.347
First, we create or load a DataFrame.

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:13.362
In real projects, this may come from a CSV file, an Excel file, a database query,

00:00:13.362 --> 00:00:18.467
or another application. Second, we inspect the data.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:22.737
Before changing anything, we need to understand what we received.

00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:28.527
We look at sample rows, the shape of the table, column names, and data types.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:35.452
Third, we select the rows and columns that are relevant to our question.

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:37.617
Then we clean the data.

00:00:37.617 --> 00:00:42.697
This may include fixing types, handling missing values, removing true duplicates, and standardizing text.

00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:47.942
After cleaning, we can transform the data.

00:00:47.942 --> 00:00:54.547
We might create a profit column, extract the month from a date, or classify transactions into size categories.

00:00:54.547 --> 00:01:01.927
Next, we summarize the data using tools such as groupby, agg, pivot_table, and crosstab.

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:08.567
If information is spread across multiple tables, we combine them using merge or concat.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.937
Finally, we validate.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:15.457
We compare row counts, inspect keys, check totals, and make sure our assumptions still hold.

00:01:15.457 --> 00:01:23.647
This sequence is useful because it gives structure to an otherwise messy analytical problem.
